elastic-rule-b26013be.limits_and_boundary:
When the magnetic field 

\bf{B}


is uniform (i.e., its gradient is zero), the term 


(\bf{B} · \nabla)\bf{B}


 becomes zero, and thus the magnetic tension force 

\bf{t}


is zero. This indicates that uniform magnetic fields do not experience tension. Conversely, as the curvature of the magnetic field lines increases, or as the magnetic field strength 

\bf{B}


 increases, the magnetic tension force 

\bf{t}


 becomes larger, signifying a stronger tendency for the field lines to straighten. If 

\bf{B}


 approaches zero, the tension force also approaches zero, as there is no magnetic field to exert tension.

